Title: THE VOICE OF ONE
Credit: Written by Dale Joseph
Author: Dale Joseph
Source: from the Voice of One canon
Draft date: 2026-08-18
Contact: The Emergence Institute

====

FADE IN:

INT. WEST WING ANNEX - ANALYST BULLPEN - DAY

Fluorescent. No windows that open. Phones ringing somewhere they can be ignored.

SANDI CARMICHAEL (30s) at a desk that knows her name. Communications, data, behavioral science — the plaque by the door says all three like they are one thing.

On her screen:

    Subject: RE: Emerging Threat Narrative — "One"

She almost deletes it.

A flicker. Finger on trash. Then stops.

The file opens. Scraped scrolls, threads, half-indexed manifestos. WRITINGS.

She reads.

COLLEAGUE passes, coffee in hand. Mid-40s, comms director's cadence.

COLLEAGUE
Who's One.

SANDI
(reading)
Failed actor. Minor following. Increasingly
coherent critique. That's what the
cover sheet wants me to say.

COLLEAGUE
Is he noise.

SANDI
He's not trying to be a candidate.
He's not trying to build a party.

She scrolls. Donor decks without the branding. Plain language underneath.

SANDI (CONT'D)
He's attacking assumptions. Markets.
Media. Us. The opposition. Same
paragraph.

COLLEAGUE
Exploitable.

SANDI
That's the box they gave me. Weaknesses.
Amplification risk. Neutralization
potential.

COLLEAGUE
And.

SANDI
And if you starve him of oxygen he
doesn't go away. You train the next
one to expect it.

Colleague waits for the useful sentence. She knows what it wants to be.

She opens her draft summary. Types:

    Subject shows high systems literacy.
    Language accessible, not academic.
    Potential for mass resonance if
    platform grows.

She stops. Cursor blinks.

There is a sharper insight. She has it. We see her have it — the way he refuses every available script, including the one where he wins by becoming what he hates.

She does not write it.

She deletes a half-sentence. Closes the omission into clean prose.

COLLEAGUE
You sending that.

SANDI
In a minute.

Colleague moves on. Sandi sits a beat longer with what she kept out.

She hits send on the thinner version. Keeps the file.

Her face: first complicity acknowledged, not performed.

CUT TO:

INT. SANDI'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

WEEKS LATER.

Small. Lived-in. No staging for anyone coming.

A kettle just off boil. ONE (50s) fills two mugs. He is careful with them.

SANDI watches, half in the kitchen doorway, sleeves pushed up.

On the counter: a vinyl sleeve, not collector-priced. The player turns low. Room, season, music — not mythology.

ONE
You take it with —

SANDI
You already know.

ONE hands it over. Their hands touch a second too long because no one is watching, and because someone could be.

Through the window: the street below, ordinary. A parked car that has been there since afternoon. No one looks up at it. Sandi does, once, then looks away.

SANDI (CONT'D)
(quiet)
You make tea like you're apologizing
to the cup.

ONE
I am.

She smiles. Moves to him. Leans against the counter beside him, not in front of him.

They stand there. Shoulder, mug, song, steam. A small domestic negotiation — who moves first when nothing is required.

No grand lines. The room does it.

SANDI
Seasons changed while we weren't
looking.

ONE
They do that.

They listen to the music a beat longer than is efficient.

CUT TO:

INT. ONE'S KITCHEN - NIGHT

Phone. Late. The kitchen that holds a life that has not been upgraded.

ONE alone, at the table. Before him: a soup can, label faced out to read English. Another behind it.

He dials. Three rings.

MOTHER (V.O.)
(filtered)
Allo?

ONE
Manman. It's me.

MOTHER (V.O.)
I know it's you. You the only one who
calls this late and don't start with
small talk.

ONE
(half-smile)
I need to tell you something.

MOTHER (V.O.)
I already know.

ONE
How —

MOTHER (V.O.)
The whole world knows, cheri. You think
I don't have a phone? The Haitian
ladies at church send me every picture.

ONE looks at the soup cans. The labels that taught English.

ONE
He said things. About you. About us.

MOTHER (V.O.)
What kind of things.

ONE
He called you illegal. Said you broke
the law. Took jobs from people who
'were born here.'

A beat. Then — tired, not bitter.

MOTHER (V.O.)
Cheri — I been called worse by better
men.

ONE
That doesn't make it okay.

MOTHER (V.O.)
No. It don't. But it also don't
surprise me.

He listens. The radiator clicks.

MOTHER (V.O.) (CONT'D)
When I first came, I worked for a man
just like him. Big house. Nice car.
Talked about values and hard work. Paid
me three dollars an hour. Cash in an
envelope. Friday.

ONE
And when you asked for a raise?

MOTHER (V.O.)
'If you don't like it, go back.' So I
quit. Found another family. Still under
the table. But they treated me like a
person.

She exhales.

MOTHER (V.O.) (CONT'D)
That man ran for city council. Won.
Talked about protecting jobs for real
Americans.

ONE's hand around the phone tightens, then loosens. He looks at the can again — price, import sticker, the small accounting of staying.

ONE
I keep a ledger. Who we owe.

MOTHER (V.O.)
You keep it because the priest stopped
putting money in the basket?

ONE
I stopped putting money in the basket.
You did first.

MOTHER (V.O.)
(soft)
I pay taxes they tell me I don't deserve
to pay. I follow laws they didn't write
for me. I will not fund men who preach
mercy on Sunday and call immigration
on Monday.

Beat.

MOTHER (V.O.) (CONT'D)
You okay?

ONE
I don't know.

MOTHER (V.O.)
Liar. You sound like you carrying the
whole world.

ONE
(quiet)
You carrying your world. That's
different.

MOTHER (V.O.)
Tell me about the girl.

ONE
She's strong. Stronger than me. She
stood up to her father. For us.

MOTHER (V.O.)
Good. She have to be strong. She
carrying your child into a war she
didn't start.

Silence. The question lands without being asked.

MOTHER (V.O.) (CONT'D)
And you? You ready to be a father?

ONE
(beat)
I'm scared I'll turn them into a
symbol instead of a person.

MOTHER (V.O.)
Then don't. Tell them the truth. Let
them see you scared. Let them see you
fail. Being human is enough.

She lets that sit. Soup-can kitchen, immigrant ledger, basket money — no speech about Haiti, just price tags and envelopes.

MOTHER (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Bring that girl to see me. Before the
baby comes.

ONE
I will.

MOTHER (V.O.)
Now go to bed. You sound like you been
crying.

ONE
I haven't —

MOTHER (V.O.)
Liar. Go to bed.

She hangs up before he can correct her.

ONE sits in the dark, phone in hand, ledger unspoken.

CUT TO:

INT. BATHROOM - NIGHT

SANDI alone. Door closed. Fan humming bad white noise.

On the sink: a test. Face-up. No hand hovering to recount.

Two lines.

No spin. No recount. No music stinger.

Hold.

SANDI breathes. Four-count, like therapy after the second attempt on her father, which was also training for secrecy.

In. Two. Three. Four. Hold. Two. Three. Four. Out.

Her hand goes to her stomach. Not performative. Searching.

Stillness, then weight. One hand around plastic that redraws the map.

She sits on the closed toilet lid. Looks at her hands. Tremble, then steady.

CUT TO:

INT. ONE AND SANDI'S KITCHEN - NIGHT

A glass sweating. Night that has been negotiated, not romanticized.

SANDI across from ONE at the kitchen table. She has set the test face-down, not hidden.

SANDI
I'm pregnant.

ONE hears it. Stands slowly, like gravity doubled.

ONE
Okay.

SANDI
My first thought was leverage. I hated
that it was.

ONE
Okay. That's allowed. Do you —

SANDI
Do I want this? I don't know.

He nods. That answer is allowed too.

SANDI (CONT'D)
My father will see succession. You will
see prophecy. The training says: name
it before someone else does.

ONE
And the training is —

SANDI
My father will see a dynastic asset.
You — if you're not careful — will see
a prophecy with a due date.

ONE starts to answer. She stops him — not unkind, but firm.

SANDI (CONT'D)
No one will see a person but me. That
child is a person, not a prophecy.

Beat. That line is boundary, not slogan.

SANDI (CONT'D)
And not an asset. Not a bridge. Not
succession. A person.

ONE hears the instruction inside the refusal. He is humbled.

ONE
What do you need.

SANDI
No names in any circle material. No
images. Ever. We decide who sleeps
where before anyone else decides for us.

She draws that line on the table with a finger, literal.

SANDI (CONT'D)
This is the line. On this side, we are
two people. On that side, they make us
into whatever story lets them keep
governing.

ONE
Then we hold this side.

CUT TO:

INT. WHITE HOUSE - PRIVATE STUDY - NIGHT

CARMICHAEL alone. Screens.

No meeting. No staff to perform for. A private office, lamp low.

On mute: unpublished stills. Brooklyn clinic exterior, winter. A figure at the door — SANDI, mid-step, coat, not posing. Another frame: ONE at a distance. Time-stamped, not yet leaked.

He watches without sound first. Always distance before feeling.

He clicks. Frame holds. Studies: what story does this let me curate.

The remote in his hand. Thumb on the button. Not yet pressed.

No speech. No music. A man deciding whether to deploy an image he already holds. The choice is visible because he does not make it yet.

He sets the remote down. Stands. Walks to the window. Beyond the gates, the country isn't immaculate.

He does not call anyone. The file and the photo are now coupled on his desk, not yet in the world.

HOLD on the still, muted.

CUT TO:

EXT. QUIET STREET - NIGHT

ONE and SANDI walk. No phones. That is the logistics.

Houses dark. A streetlight humming.

SANDI
No recording. No circle material with
names or images. Ever.

ONE
Ever.

SANDI
What pregnancy can carry publicly —
doctor visits that leak, the kind of
coat that photographs. Who sleeps where
when they watch the block.

ONE
Love as logistics.

SANDI
Love vs. mission as shared work. Not a
sacrifice speech.

They walk. Pace unhurried, conversation not for anyone else.

SANDI (CONT'D)
We don't make the child into story.
We don't let circles make family into
emblem. If they want a face, they get
method. Not us.

ONE
Method over charisma.

SANDI
And if they push 'our future' —

ONE
— we say 'her future.'

SANDI looks at him. Nods. The pronoun is strategy and boundary at once.

They keep walking. Consensus that will be tested.

CUT TO:

INT. WEST WING ANNEX - SANDI'S OFFICE - DAY

Daylight that costs standing.

SANDI at her desk. Phone to ear. No speaker.

AIDE (V.O.)
(filtered)
They have it. Clinic. Brooklyn. Not
published yet. They're deciding how to
use it.

SANDI
Who told you.

AIDE (V.O.)
Someone who thought you should hear it
before the statement is drafted for you.

She looks at the tablet on her desk — a draft statement already formatted in West Wing font, her name in first person so she can 'make it her own.'

She reads the poison in it: 'our future,' 'family,' 'dialogue.'

She picks up a pen. Crosses. Not tweaking — amputating.

She answers, no spin, voice level:

SANDI
She's focused on her health and her
future.

Beat. The word is the line.

SANDI (CONT'D)
Not 'our.' Her.

AIDE (V.O.)
They won't like that.

SANDI
They're not supposed to. They decide
whether they want my name or their
words. They can't have both.

She hangs up. Locks the statement.

CUT TO:

INT. ONE'S KITCHEN TABLE - NIGHT

ONE alone, pen, radio low. No laptop — paper.

He writes. Reads aloud to the empty room, testing weight.

ONE
(reading, tired authority)
'I have debated men like him my whole
life at kitchen tables, in unemployment
lines — I won't debate him on a
donors' stage.'

He underlines kitchen tables. Adds, without rhetoric:

ONE (CONT'D)
'If you seek truth — Release the
Baptist. Publish the evidence. Allow
independent verification.'

He stops. Looks at the last line he has not yet written. Then writes it:

    'Stand without pre-approved questions,
     without green rooms, without
     microphones only your friends can cut.'

He disables nothing — this is paper first, then post. He folds it. Photographs it plain, background blank, text-only.

He posts the same paragraph — refusal as offer. Comments disabled, as before.

INSERT — SCREEN: the post, white background. No face.

ONE reads it once more to the empty kitchen. It is not a performance.

CUT TO:

INT. WHITE HOUSE - PRIVATE STUDY - NIGHT

The same room as before. Now an AIDE reads the essay aloud from a printout. Flat.

AIDE
'I have debated men like him my whole
life at kitchen tables — I won't debate
him on a donors' stage —'

CARMICHAEL listens, still. The third refusal in three registers: post, paragraph, terms.

The AIDE finishes.

Silence. Then:

CARMICHAEL
Get me the continuities.

AIDE
Sir —

CARMICHAEL
Not the usual staff. The other meeting.

The AIDE understands. Exits.

CARMICHAEL alone, the verification offer on his desk, the muted stills still there, the essay on top of them.

He has been refused the stage, refused the face, refused the spectacle. The causal turn is quiet: continue refusing will require manufacturing a voice he could not capture.

FADE OUT.

